  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\n\nCentral idea:\nWe develop a novel optimization strategy based on adaptive piecewise polynomial interpolation to improve the search quality and efficiency in the high-dimensional space. The core innovations are:\n\n1. A multi-resolution adaptive search that starts from low dimensions and gradually increases\n2. Piecewise polynomial parameterization to reduce the optimization dimensionality\n3. Dynamic upsampling strategy that preserves function properties\n4. Progressive optimization across resolutions and scales\n5. Topology-aware search that leverages the structure of the solution space\n\nThe approach moves beyond fixed resolution optimization by:\n- Starting with low-dimensional coarse representations that can be refined\n- Using polynomial basis functions to represent key pieces of the solution\n- Dynamically adjusting complexity based on fitness landscape features\n- Maintaining a library of validated decision rules across scales\n\nOur assumption is that locally optimal solutions in lower dimensions can be extended to higher dimensions while outperforming the single-resolution approach.\n\nMathematical specification\n\nRepresentation:\nWe represent h(x) using a piecewise polynomial basis:\n  h(x) = \u03a3_{p=0}^{m} c_p * \u03a6_p(x)\nwhere \u03a6_p are basis functions (LSH, wavelets, or control polynomials) and c_p are coefficients to be optimized.\n\nObjective decomposition:\n  C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \n  = max_k [ \u03a3_{i,j} h_i (1-h_j) \u03b4(i-j-k) ] dx\n\nBut we transform this to a vector objective:\n  C5_vec = [ max_k corr(h, 1-h, k) for k in K] * dx\n\nConstraints:\n  \u2211_i h_i = n/2.0\n  0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 \u2200i\n\nParameterization:\n  We use a lifted basis function representation:\n    Let f(x) = [\u03a6_0(x), \u03a6_1(x), ..., \u03a6_m(x)]^T\n    Then h(x) = c^T f(x) + b\n\nwith constraints enforced through barrier functions and repair mechanisms.\n\nImplementation procedure\n\nStage 1: Parameterization and initialization\n- Input: seed, budget, initial_h_values (optional)\n- Initialize random number generator\n- Validate input size ntry = n_points if not provided\n- If initial_h_values available, downsample to base resolution n_base, else generate uniform\n- Initialize basis functions (Polynomial, Fourier, or Wavelets) based on input size\n- Initialize coefficients uniformly with clipping\n\nAlgorithm Parameters:\n- Stage 1: Low Dimension (world)\n  - n_low = 1000 (lowest resolution)\n  - Polynomial degree = 4\n  - Max evaluations = 2000\n- Stage 2: Medium Dimension\n  - n_med = n_low * 2\n  - Polynomial degree = 5\n  - Max evaluations = 4000\n- Stage 3: High Dimension\n  - n_high = n_med * 2\n  - Polynomial degree = 6\n  - Max evaluations = Linear in budget\n\nAdaptive Basis Construction:\n  - Forward selection of basis functions\n  - Cross-validated selection (leave-ones-out)\n  - Fitness landscape-based selection\n  - Local geometry-informed selection\n\nObjective Function:\n  - Evaluate C5 constraint\n  - Use L1/L2 penalty for constraints\n  - Repair mechanism with precision control\n\nData Structures:\n  - Use a priority queue for candidate selection\n  - Use a genetic algorithm for factorization\n  - Maintain library of cached function values and intermediate results\n\nTermination Criteria:\n- Maximum evaluations per level from budget\n- Reduction plateau detected\n- Fit streak detected\n- Reproducible improvement\n\nSearch choices and variations\n\nWe implement three configurations:\n1. Polynomial Basis with Chebyshev Polynomials\n2. Fourier Series with Phase-Varying Basis\n3. Wavelet Packet Decomposition\n\nThe main loop:\n\n  Initialize population with repair.aimAccess the initial h_values = initial_h_values if provided\n\n  For depth from 1 to 3 (each doubling resolution):\n    - Generate new candidates at current resolution\n    - Apply repair for constraint satisfaction\n    - Evaluate their fits\n    - Keep the best candidates across all resolutions\n    - Up-sample the best of each resolution by polynomial interpolation\n\n  Global Selection Strategy:\n\n    GlobalBest = None\n    LocalBest = None\n\n    For each resolution level r:\n      For each candidate:\n        if new_unfitness_improvement(candidate):\n          accept candidate\n          if candidate > old_global_best:\n            mark global improvement\n        else:\n          probabilistic transmutation\n\n  Up-sampling:\n    if current_n < target_n:\n      use Lagrange interpolation\n      enforce sum-distribution matching\n      probabilistically cap to [0,1]\n\n  Termination Trigger:\n\n    if time_remaining <= 10 or no_improvement > 10:\n        break\n\n    if global_improvement streak > 20:\n        restart from best without constraint enforcement\n\n  Multiple Basis Representation technique for progressive refinement:\n\n    Base Representation (starting level):\n      f0 = [c0, c1, ..., c_m] at resolution r\n\n    Progressive Refinement:\n      f_{m+1} = f0 + \u0394c * random_vector + \u03bb * ring_filtration\n\n    Filter selection:\n      Gauss filter with width \u03c3\n      High-pass filter on wavenumbers\n      using Fast Fourier Transform\n\n  Repair Function:\n    def repair_basis(alpha=0.1):\n        h = np.clip(basis_output(x).astype(float), 0.0, 1.0)\n        target_sum = current_n / 2.0\n        offset = (target_sum - np.sum(h)) / current_n * alpha\n        h = np.clip(h + offset, 0.0, 1.0)\n        return h\n\nComputational budget and robustness\n\nEstimated run time for each resolution level:\n\n  Level 1 (lowest): ~30 seconds\n  Level 2: ~200 seconds\n  Level 3: ~1000 seconds\n\nTotal with budget of 1300 seconds we can complete:\n\n  Level 1 + Level 2 + Partial Level 3\n\nWe use dynamic resource allocation:\n\n  For each level, allocate a fixed percentage of the remaining budget\n  For low dimension: 40% budget\n  Medium: 50%\n  High: 10%\n\nImplementation specifics:\n\n  We use a quadratic incomplete Cholesky factorization to handle the high-dimensional constraint\n  employ projected gradients to enforce the equality\n  use low-rank approximations where possible\n\nValidation and return contract\n\nPre-check candidate for:\n\n  - Finiteness of objective\n  - Sum constraint epsilon 1e-6 level\n  - [0,1] bounds for all values\n  - Recent improvement record\n\nFinality criteria:\n\n  if timeremain <= 10: return best candidate\n  if improvement rate < 0.001: return best candidate\n\nThe return will be:\n  (h_values_array, c5_bound, n_points)\n\nAdhere to the Erdos checks:\n\n  1. Check sum_constraint: assert np.allclose(np.sum(h_values), n_points/2.0, atol=1e-6)\n  2. Check 0<=h<=1: assert all h_values in [0,1]\n  3. Check training-sized upsample if available: \n        if initial_h_values is not None: \n            check_redistribution(initial_h_values, h_values)\n\n  4. Recompute objective with verify: \n        c5_bound_2 = compute_c5(h_values)\n        if abs(c5_bound_2 - c5_bound) > 1e-6:\n            raise contradiction\n\n  5. Check runtime environment: only numpy, scipy, and allowed solvers.\n\nOur default runs are with:\n\n  n_points_low = 512\n  n_points_med = 1024\n  n_points_high = 2048\n\nWe choose polynomial basis in the beginning that satisfy:\n  sum_basis_constraint(\u03a6) = n_points/2.0\n\nfor basis vectors allow piecwise constancy.\n\nLet's now implement the code accordingly.",
